Assume $X$ is countable with enumeration $X = \{p_i\}_{i=1}^\infty$. Assume $X$ is discrete. Fix an ideal $\mathcal{I} \subseteq \mathcal{C}\ell\left(X\right)$ such that $\mathcal{F}in \subseteq \mathcal{I}$ and $R^{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{F}in}$ is injective. Set $\mathcal{J} := \mathrm{ulm}\left(\mathcal{I}\right)$ so that Theorem 6.13 implies $R^{\mathcal{J}}_{\mathcal{F}in}$ is also injective (for example, $\mathcal{I} = \mathcal{F}in_n$ and $\mathcal{J} = \mathcal{F}in_{n+1}$). For any collection of radii $\{r_p\}_{p \in X}$ with $r_p \in (0,\mathrm{rad}_{\mathcal{J}}\left(p\right))$, $u \in \LF{\mathcal{I}}{X}$ such that $\supp{u} \cap B_{r_p}(p)$ is finite for all $p \in X$, sequence $v_n \in \LF{\mathcal{J}}{X}$ such that $\sup_n \|v_n\|_{\mathcal{J}} < 1$ and $R^{\mathcal{J}}_{\mathcal{I}}(v_n) \to u$, and $c \in (0,1)$, there exists another sequence $\tilde{v}_n \in \LF{\mathcal{J}}{X}$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item $\|\tilde{v}_n\|_{\mathcal{J}} < 1$ for every $n \geq 1$,
\item $R^{\mathcal{J}}_{\mathcal{I}}(\tilde{v}_n) \to u$,
\item for all $p_i \in X$ and $n \geq i$, $\supp{\tilde{v}_n} \cap B_{cr_{p_i}}(p_i) \subseteq \supp{u} \cap B_{cr_{p_i}}(p_i)$.
\end{itemize}
